387
00:39:17,940 --> 00:39:23,710
from the landlord James Warden, who was taking
Tesla to court for not paying property tax,

388
00:39:23,710 --> 00:39:28,920
as well as another bill from the phone company
who had installed a special line out of the

389
00:39:28,920 --> 00:39:34,580
laboratory, Tesla struggled financially. Tesla’s
promise to extend the coverage of his system

390
00:39:34,580 --> 00:39:40,381
so that it could be detected as far away as
Australia failed to persuade Morgan, who in

391
00:39:40,381 --> 00:39:47,910
July 1903 let Tesla know that he was unwilling
to invest any more capital, a mortal and unexpected

392
00:39:47,910 --> 00:39:55,100
blow to the World Telegraphy System that enraged
Tesla, who in true mad scientist fashion furiously

393
00:39:55,100 --> 00:40:01,720
cranked up the voltage of the magnifying transmitter
at Wardenclyffe to the maximum level and hurled

394
00:40:01,720 --> 00:40:07,440
lightning bolts into the New York sky. From
a risk perspective Morgan’s decision was

395
00:40:07,440 --> 00:40:13,050
quite understandable, since after 2 and a
half years Tesla had failed to fulfill his

396
00:40:13,050 --> 00:40:20,000
pledge to provide a transatlantic system in
6 to 8 months and a Pacific branch a year

397
00:40:20,000 --> 00:40:25,870
later, yet others have postulated that Morgan
abandoned Wardenclyffe because he was worried

398
00:40:25,870 --> 00:40:32,260
that Tesla was going to make the energy completely
free for everyone and thus deprive his firm

399
00:40:32,260 --> 00:40:38,690
of a lucrative paycheck, while another interpretation
contests that Morgan was growing increasingly

400
00:40:38,690 --> 00:40:44,250
less confident in the wireless industry, which
had been mired in scandal by the actions of

401
00:40:44,250 --> 00:40:50,790
Lancelot E. Pike. He was a conman who had
stolen investor money after promising to create

402
00:40:50,790 --> 00:40:57,119
a wireless service between Philadelphia and
New York. Moreover, Morgan felt more inclined

403
00:40:57,119 --> 00:41:03,079
to invest in Deforest Wireless automobiles,
a company founded by another of Tesla’s

404
00:41:03,079 --> 00:41:08,480
rivals that was projected to make 5 million
dollars a year, and which had already secured

405
00:41:08,480 --> 00:41:16,250
for itself a major contract with the US Navy
by February 1903 supplying wireless De Forest

406
00:41:16,250 --> 00:41:17,980
transmitters.

407
00:41:17,980 --> 00:41:23,050
After this, Tesla, no longer the darling of
electrical engineering, continued to have

408
00:41:23,050 --> 00:41:29,180
no luck attracting investors as the breakdown
of his Wardenclyffe project had turned academic

409
00:41:29,180 --> 00:41:35,280
and public opinion against him, with the once
celebrated genius, now painted as a man who

410
00:41:35,280 --> 00:41:41,550
could never quite fulfill his promises. Determined
to restore his credibility to investors by

411
00:41:41,550 --> 00:41:48,119
putting out a commercial product, Tesla devised
a scheme to sell small Tesla coils to laboratories

412
00:41:48,119 --> 00:41:54,690
around the country, via a company called the
Tesla Electric and Manufacturing Company but

413
00:41:54,690 --> 00:42:00,390
this ultimately went under because of a lack
of investment. Downtrodden and defamed in

414
00:42:00,390 --> 00:42:06,880
the US, Tesla would learn that even his reputation
in his homeland could not save him, after

415
00:42:06,880 --> 00:42:13,609
Serbian bankers declined to fund him, while
his old business partner John Jacob Astor,

416
00:42:13,609 --> 00:42:20,329
still annoyed that Tesla had spent his 1899
loan researching terrestrial stationary waves

417
00:42:20,329 --> 00:42:26,960
instead of wireless lighting, politely refused
to get involved. By early 1904, Tesla was

418
00:42:26,960 --> 00:42:32,860
hiring out his services as a consultant and
had embarked on a new project that aimed to

419
00:42:32,860 --> 00:42:39,160
harness the power of Niagara Falls in partnership
with the businessman William B. Rankine, yet

420
00:42:39,160 --> 00:42:45,000
Tesla’s real desire was always to restart
his experiments at Wardenclyffe, a prospect

421
00:42:45,000 --> 00:42:50,710
that was becoming increasingly unlikely since
investors were discouraged by the fact that

422
00:42:50,710 --> 00:42:56,350
Morgan still owned 51% of Tesla’s patents,
meaning that business partners would always

423
00:42:56,350 --> 00:43:02,760
have to consult him to reap any financial
benefit from Tesla’s inventions. In response,

424
00:43:02,760 --> 00:43:08,680
Tesla bombarded Morgan with impassioned appeals
to take him back on, in a series of letters

425
00:43:08,680 --> 00:43:14,910
that could come in many forms, with some carefully
prepared proposals promising Morgan unrealistically

426
00:43:14,910 --> 00:43:21,160
high profit margins, while in others Tesla
desperately scribbled emotional outbursts

427
00:43:21,160 --> 00:43:24,800
decrying his unfair treatment.

428
00:43:24,800 --> 00:43:30,099
Unable to drum up financial backing and still
struggling to get the Wardenclyffe tower functioning,

429
00:43:30,099 --> 00:43:36,440
Tesla was in a dark place and began spiraling
into a true nervous breakdown after the untimely

430
00:43:36,440 --> 00:43:41,839
death of his business associate Rankine and
the collapse of Canadian Niagara Power in

431
00:43:41,839 --> 00:43:48,849
the fall of 1905. A man who was always fascinated
with human psychology, in the 1920s Tesla

432
00:43:48,849 --> 00:43:54,330
shared his experience of emotional collapse
with author George Sylvester Viereck in a

433
00:43:54,330 --> 00:44:00,950
well-publicized book on Freudian theory, explaining
how in his delirium, he was haunted by images

434
00:44:00,950 --> 00:44:06,720
of his deceased brother Dane as well as their
mother. Tesla would recover from his breakdown

435
00:44:06,720 --> 00:44:13,640
in 1906, and with a new determination to prove
himself as a valuable asset, he switched his

436
00:44:13,640 --> 00:44:19,700
focus from electrical to mechanical engineering,
delving into the science of flight in a bid

437
00:44:19,700 --> 00:44:26,270
to raise money so that he could continue his
work at Wardenclyffe, which in 1904, due to

438
00:44:26,270 --> 00:44:31,440
lack of funds, he had mortgaged to George
C. Boldt, the owner of the Waldorf-Astoria

439
00:44:31,440 --> 00:44:37,150
Hotel where Tesla lived for many years. The
result was the invention of a turbine that

440
00:44:37,150 --> 00:44:43,869
worked without blades, but Tesla’s contraption
was met less than enthusiastically by John

441
00:44:43,869 --> 00:44:50,390
James Astor, who refused to invest in the
idea in 1909, yet Tesla still remained highly

442
00:44:50,390 --> 00:44:56,920
confident in his design, issuing two patents
for a pump and a turbine in October and even

443
00:44:56,920 --> 00:45:02,760
starting a new company, the Tesla Propulsion
Company. However, after Tesla again failed

444
00:45:02,760 --> 00:45:08,869
to impress at a demonstration at the Waterside
Power Station in New York, and still daydreaming

445
00:45:08,869 --> 00:45:14,970
about restarting his research into wireless
power, he approached the son of J.P Morgan,

446
00:45:14,970 --> 00:45:21,350
Jack Morgan, in 1913 about him becoming a
potential investor. More interested in his

447
00:45:21,350 --> 00:45:28,369
turbine designs, Jack Morgan allocated Tesla
20,000 dollars, who instead of using the money

448
00:45:28,369 --> 00:45:34,730
in the way it was intended, spent it all trying
to persuade Sigmund Bergmann, a German industrialist,

449
00:45:34,730 --> 00:45:41,579
to finance his project, which was discontinued
after the outbreak of World War One.

450
00:45:41,579 --> 00:45:47,480
Over the next 10 years, as his personal wealth
began to evaporate, Tesla continued to assess

451
00:45:47,480 --> 00:45:53,670
his turbine and to work in conjunction with
manufacturers such as Pyle National in Chicago,

452
00:45:53,670 --> 00:45:58,930
Allis-Chalmers in Milwaukee, and the Budd
company in Philadelphia, but he was unable

453
00:45:58,930 --> 00:46:04,540
to fix a recurring problem that ultimately
spelled the project’s doom, as if operated

454
00:46:04,540 --> 00:46:11,200
at over 10,000 rpm, the thin disks of the
internal machinery would start to deform,

455
00:46:11,200 --> 00:46:16,280
and there were no stronger materials available.
Tesla’s inability to secure funding for

456
00:46:16,280 --> 00:46:21,530
any of his pursuits, and his penchant for
overspending, led to the near emptying of

457
00:46:21,530 --> 00:46:27,920
his bank accounts and from 1916, he was forced
to file for bankruptcy. Tesla admitted that

458
00:46:27,920 --> 00:46:34,540
his monthly income was no more than 350 to
400 dollars after declaring he was unable

459
00:46:34,540 --> 00:46:41,290
to pay 935 dollars in taxes to the New York
treasury, and although he was still technically

460
00:46:41,290 --> 00:46:46,770
the president and treasurer of the Nikola
Tesla Company, over 90% of the company’s

461
00:46:46,770 --> 00:46:53,470
stock was owned by friends, bankers, and creditors,
and many of his patents by then had expired,

462
00:46:53,470 --> 00:46:59,569
leaving Tesla virtually penniless. In a bid
to save himself from financial ruin, Tesla

463
00:46:59,569 --> 00:47:06,780
launched a lawsuit against Marconi in August
1915, accusing the Italian of illegally patenting

464
00:47:06,780 --> 00:47:13,510
radio technology in 1904, but the case went
nowhere and Marconi remained the official

465
00:47:13,510 --> 00:47:20,930
owner for the time being, with the decision
later overturned by the Supreme Court in 1943

466
00:47:20,930 --> 00:47:24,180
only a few months after Tesla’s death.

467
00:47:24,180 --> 00:47:29,850
Consequently, Tesla was forced to earn a living
by creating minor inventions and registering

468
00:47:29,850 --> 00:47:36,010
several improvements in automobile speedometers,
frequency meters, and flow meters which he

469
00:47:36,010 --> 00:47:42,809
licensed to Waltham Watch Company in 1918.
The company used Tesla’s name as a marketing

470
00:47:42,809 --> 00:47:49,089
ploy to sell ‘scientifically built speedometers’,
as despite Tesla’s changing circumstances,

471
00:47:49,089 --> 00:47:54,369
his name still carried a certain gravitas,
and Tesla’s opinions and thoughts still

472
00:47:54,369 --> 00:48:01,520
garnered much press interest, such as in 1917
when, presaging the advent of radar technology

473
00:48:01,520 --> 00:48:08,290
in the 1930s, he forecast that microwave radiation
could be employed to detect ships. In the

474
00:48:08,290 --> 00:48:14,829
midst of his financial troubles, Tesla still
had his admirers, and in 1915 it was reported

475
00:48:14,829 --> 00:48:20,470
by the New York Times that he had jointly
won the Nobel Prize for Physics, an honor

476
00:48:20,470 --> 00:48:26,369
he was to share with his one-time ally and
now bitter enemy Thomas Edison, but when it

477
00:48:26,369 --> 00:48:31,790
was presented at the awards ceremony it was
instead given to William H. Bragg and his

478
00:48:31,790 --> 00:48:38,180
son, as Tesla refused to share the award with
his nemesis nor had he forgiven the institute

479
00:48:38,180 --> 00:48:43,730
for recognizing Marconi as the pioneer of
radio communication technology after they

480
00:48:43,730 --> 00:48:51,470
had awarded him the same accolade in 1909.
Two years later in 1917 however, Tesla’s

481
00:48:51,470 --> 00:48:56,460
individual efforts would be recognized when
he accepted the Edison Medal from the American

482
00:48:56,460 --> 00:49:02,890
Institute of Electrical Engineers, the most
prestigious prize in electronic engineering,

483
00:49:02,890 --> 00:49:08,440
where his life’s work was praised in an
address by the vice-president of the organization,

484
00:49:08,440 --> 00:49:09,440
who said:

485
00:49:09,440 --> 00:49:14,880
“Were we to seize and eliminate from our
industrial world the result of Mr. Tesla's

486
00:49:14,880 --> 00:49:21,440
work, the wheels of industry would cease to
turn, our electric cars and trains would stop,

487
00:49:21,440 --> 00:49:28,339
our towns would be dark and our mills would
be idle and dead. His name marks an epoch

488
00:49:28,339 --> 00:49:31,430
in the advance of electrical science.”

489
00:49:31,430 --> 00:49:36,770
Yet Tesla cared very little for the decoration,
opting to leave the Engineer’s Club where

490
00:49:36,770 --> 00:49:42,500
the awards ceremony was taking place shortly
before he was presented with the medallion,

491
00:49:42,500 --> 00:49:47,510
forcing his friend and the person who had
nominated him, B.A Behrend to embark on a

492
00:49:47,510 --> 00:49:53,300
frantic search that ended in Bryant Park,
across from the venue, where Tesla was busy

493
00:49:53,300 --> 00:49:59,730
feeding pigeons. Tesla particularly loathed
the fact that the award was in his rival’s

494
00:49:59,730 --> 00:50:05,350
name. Despite being lauded for his work in
electricity, Nikola Tesla remained poor throughout

495
00:50:05,350 --> 00:50:10,880
the 1920s, and because he made only a small
income from the royalties he received from

496
00:50:10,880 --> 00:50:17,350
licensing his minor inventions he became embroiled
in a string of legal disputes, such as in

497
00:50:17,350 --> 00:50:25,260
June 1925 when he was sued by the attorney
Ralph J. Hawkins for failing to pay 913 dollars

498
00:50:25,260 --> 00:50:31,930
in fees. It was during this decade that Tesla
started to retreat from the public eye, living

499
00:50:31,930 --> 00:50:37,599
hotel to hotel as an eccentric recluse and
spending so much of his time feeding the pigeons

500
00:50:37,599 --> 00:50:43,690
in Bryant Park behind the New York Public
Library, that one end of the park, Nikola

501
00:50:43,690 --> 00:50:46,890
Tesla Corner, now honors his name.

502
00:50:46,890 --> 00:50:54,549
However, on his 75th birthday in 1931 Nikola
Tesla was suddenly back in the spotlight,

503
00:50:54,549 --> 00:50:59,260
appearing on the front cover of Time magazine,
and thanks to the efforts of a young science

504
00:50:59,260 --> 00:51:04,579
writer called Kenneth Swezey, he received
over 70 letters of congratulation published

505
00:51:04,579 --> 00:51:10,339
in a testimonial volume from some of the most
esteemed scientists of the day, including

506
00:51:10,339 --> 00:51:17,150
Albert Einstein. Interviewed by Time magazine,
Tesla outlined many of his future plans, and

507
00:51:17,150 --> 00:51:22,910
confidently disclosed how he was going to
disprove Einstein’s theory of relativity,

508
00:51:22,910 --> 00:51:28,620
how he was unconvinced that energy was released
from a split atom, as well as espousing the

509
00:51:28,620 --> 00:51:34,250
possibility of interplanetary communications
in a conversation he enjoyed so much that

510
00:51:34,250 --> 00:51:40,000
every year thereafter, Tesla would organize
a press conference on his birthday, usually

511
00:51:40,000 --> 00:51:45,700
a 6 hour affair where Tesla would speak to
a number of reporters and update them about

512
00:51:45,700 --> 00:51:51,640
his scientific and personal progress. For
example, in 1932, he informed the general

513
00:51:51,640 --> 00:51:59,569
public about his desire to build a motor propelled
by cosmic rays, and in 1936 he told journalists

514
00:51:59,569 --> 00:52:05,680
that he wiggled his toes hundreds of times
before he went to bed, an exercise he believed

515
00:52:05,680 --> 00:52:12,940
would tone his body and enable him to live
until he was 135 years old. The 1934 party

516
00:52:12,940 --> 00:52:18,740
was a particular highlight, where Tesla first
revealed he was in the process of creating

517
00:52:18,740 --> 00:52:24,010
a particle beam weapon, claiming in the New
York Times that it could:

518
00:52:24,010 --> 00:52:30,010
“…send concentrated beams of particles
through the free air, of such tremendous energy

519
00:52:30,010 --> 00:52:36,710
that they will bring down a fleet of 10,000
enemy airplanes at a distance of 250 miles

520
00:52:36,710 --> 00:52:42,119
from a defending nation’s border and will
cause armies of millions to drop dead in their

521
00:52:42,119 --> 00:52:43,150
tracks.”

522
00:52:43,150 --> 00:52:48,049
Anticipating the later doctrine of ‘Mutually
Assured Destruction’ and the concept of

523
00:52:48,049 --> 00:52:54,460
nuclear stalemate, Tesla believed his laser
beam could end war completely since it was

524
00:52:54,460 --> 00:53:00,610
so devastating that every conceivable defensive
measure would be useless against it. Although

525
00:53:00,610 --> 00:53:07,270
Tesla was susceptible to flights of fancy
on occasion, the death beam was a real project,

526
00:53:07,270 --> 00:53:14,340
and was the subject of a Tesla study rediscovered
in 1984 entitled ‘The New Art of Projecting

527
00:53:14,340 --> 00:53:20,310
Concentrated Non-dispersive Energy through
Natural Media’, which laid out the fundamentals

528
00:53:20,310 --> 00:53:27,030
of a weapons system that discharged tungsten
and mercury particles at 48 times the speed

529
00:53:27,030 --> 00:53:32,080
of sound to produce devastating projectiles.

530
00:53:32,080 --> 00:53:37,240
Despite Tesla’s invention being impossible,
it elicited a great deal of publicity, motivating

531
00:53:37,240 --> 00:53:43,630
Tesla to recruit Hungarian architect Titus
deBobula to design a new tower to evaluate

532
00:53:43,630 --> 00:53:50,240
the death beam, yet the project would never
get off the ground, as by 1935, Tesla was

533
00:53:50,240 --> 00:53:56,270
no longer working with deBobula, who had proven
himself a slippery character after attempting

534
00:53:56,270 --> 00:54:02,790
to borrow money from Tesla and trying to get
him involved in an illegal arms deal in Paraguay.

535
00:54:02,790 --> 00:54:08,810
Nevertheless Tesla, ever wily, was still able
to use the furore that surrounded him to his

536
00:54:08,810 --> 00:54:15,180
advantage in some situations, offering up
a working prototype of the laser beam, valued

537
00:54:15,180 --> 00:54:21,130
at 10,000 dollars, to the managers of the
Governor Clinton Hotel as compensation for

538
00:54:21,130 --> 00:54:26,860
the 400 dollars that he owed them, while warning
them that the box he gave them supposedly

539
00:54:26,860 --> 00:54:33,530
containing the weapon would explode if improperly
handled, resulting in terrified staff depositing

540
00:54:33,530 --> 00:54:39,560
it at the back end of the hotel vault. The
laser beam also came to the attention of the

541
00:54:39,560 --> 00:54:45,109
international community, and Tesla soon found
himself entering negotiations with the League

542
00:54:45,109 --> 00:54:51,119
of Nations, the UK government, and even the
Soviet Union, who signed a contract in April

543
00:54:51,119 --> 00:54:57,470
1935 stipulating that Tesla was to supply
them with the information necessary to construct

544
00:54:57,470 --> 00:55:03,680
the weapon, although it is unknown if Soviet
scientists actually carried out the research.

545
00:55:03,680 --> 00:55:09,910
The project however would start to disintegrate
from January 1938, after the British concluded

546
00:55:09,910 --> 00:55:16,490
negotiations with Tesla, while the final nail
in the coffin would occur in 1940, after he

547
00:55:16,490 --> 00:55:21,849
was left empty-handed following a desperate
attempt to persuade the US government that

548
00:55:21,849 --> 00:55:26,740
the laser beam could be a viable World War
Two weapon.

549
00:55:26,740 --> 00:55:31,589
Hampering Tesla in his last ditch attempts
to liaise with international governments,

550
00:55:31,589 --> 00:55:37,589
was his declining health, which had started
to deteriorate in 1937 after he was run over

551
00:55:37,589 --> 00:55:43,970
by a taxi and refused to get medical treatment
for injuries that he would never recover from.

552
00:55:43,970 --> 00:55:49,660
Subsisting on a meager diet of boiled vegetables
and warm milk, and making sure to keep three

553
00:55:49,660 --> 00:55:55,780
feet away from everyone to avoid catching
germs, Tesla’s personal health choices led

554
00:55:55,780 --> 00:56:03,470
to no improvements, and by 1942 he was spending
the majority of his days confined to his bed

555
00:56:03,470 --> 00:56:08,690
where his grip on reality started to loosen,
when in July, for example, Tesla tried to

556
00:56:08,690 --> 00:56:16,600
send money to Mark Twain who had died in 1913.
Opting to see only a select few visitors,

557
00:56:16,600 --> 00:56:22,240
including the exiled prince of Yugoslavia
Peter II and a young scientist called Bloyce

558
00:56:22,240 --> 00:56:28,100
Fitzgerald, the creator of an anti-tank gun
who would come to discuss inventions, Tesla

559
00:56:28,100 --> 00:56:31,800
isolated himself from the world as he deteriorated.

560
00:56:31,800 --> 00:56:38,380
Nikola Tesla died of a heart attack in his
sleep on the 7th of January 1943 at his executive

561
00:56:38,380 --> 00:56:45,130
suite in Room 3327 of the Hotel New Yorker,
where he had been living for the previous

562
00:56:45,130 --> 00:56:51,220
10 years. Inundated with letters of condolence
from major scientific and political figures

563
00:56:51,220 --> 00:56:56,720
all over the world, such as Eleanor Roosevelt,
the wife of the US President, the vice-president

564
00:56:56,720 --> 00:57:02,290
Henry Wallace, and a host of nobel laureates,
Tesla’s funeral took place at St John the

565
00:57:02,290 --> 00:57:08,540
Divine Cathedral in New York City on the 12th
of January, where it was attended by over

566
00:57:08,540 --> 00:57:15,160
2000 people and presided over by members of
the Serbian Orthodox Church. Afterwards Nikola

567
00:57:15,160 --> 00:57:21,630
Tesla, who by the end of his life boasted
over 700 patents to his name, was cremated

568
00:57:21,630 --> 00:57:27,609
and his ashes were placed inside a golden
sphere, which is still exhibited at the Tesla

569
00:57:27,609 --> 00:57:29,799
Museum in Belgrade.

570
00:57:29,799 --> 00:57:35,220
Following his death, the US government were
curious as to whether among Tesla’s surviving

571
00:57:35,220 --> 00:57:40,130
notes there could be information to help them
in the war effort, and so they investigated

572
00:57:40,130 --> 00:57:47,089
his documents but found nothing of profound
importance, and by 1951 they had been repatriated

573
00:57:47,089 --> 00:57:54,569
to the Tesla Museum in Belgrade and were accompanied
a few years later in 1957 by Tesla’s ashes.

574
00:57:54,569 --> 00:58:01,069
For decades after though, the mystery surrounding
the laser beam gun persisted, as the US government

575
00:58:01,069 --> 00:58:06,400
consistently denied they had in their possession
one of Tesla’s microfilms, which according

576
00:58:06,400 --> 00:58:12,349
to official records had been intensively reviewed
for over a month sometime after his death

577
00:58:12,349 --> 00:58:17,390
- the truth of these claims however remains
unproven.

578
00:58:17,390 --> 00:58:23,059
Born to Serbian parents on the Austro-Hungarian
borderlands, Nikola spent his early childhood

579
00:58:23,059 --> 00:58:29,180
in pastoral paradise, playing for hours in
the farmyard and the churchyard with his siblings

580
00:58:29,180 --> 00:58:35,160
and his cat Macack, who first set in motion
Tesla’s lifelong obsession after showing

581
00:58:35,160 --> 00:58:41,690
him the strange ways that electric phenomena
interacted with the material world. As a youngster,

582
00:58:41,690 --> 00:58:47,450
Tesla was besieged by a catalogue of perplexing
illnesses, including an oversensitivity to

583
00:58:47,450 --> 00:58:53,530
mental images which made the products of his
imagination seem real as well as a fixation

584
00:58:53,530 --> 00:59:00,500
with quantifying everything he saw and experienced.
Tesla was a classic eccentric genius, possessing

585
00:59:00,500 --> 00:59:07,349
an irrepressible self-belief in his own ability
to devise fresh and original concepts, which

586
00:59:07,349 --> 00:59:13,490
he presented with the flamboyance and performance
flair of a showman, as onstage he was equally

587
00:59:13,490 --> 00:59:19,710
as electric as his subject matter, coming
across, in the words of New York World reporter

588
00:59:19,710 --> 00:59:21,190
Arthur Brisbane, as:

589
00:59:21,190 --> 00:59:27,460
“A most radiant creature, with light flaming
at every pore of his skin, from the tips of

590
00:59:27,460 --> 00:59:32,660
his fingers and from the end of every hair
on his head”

591
00:59:32,660 --> 00:59:39,150
By the middle of the 1890s Tesla was at the
apex of his power and making major contributions

592
00:59:39,150 --> 00:59:45,480
to a number of other academic fields, but
Tesla, who prioritized his intellectual pursuits

593
00:59:45,480 --> 00:59:51,420
and never took a wife or started a family,
also had his fair share of anxieties, the

594
00:59:51,420 --> 00:59:57,500
pressure of his line of work making him particularly
susceptible to emotional outbursts of despair

595
00:59:57,500 --> 01:00:03,630
and anger when things were not going his way.
Tesla’s misfortunes often arose because

596
01:00:03,630 --> 01:00:09,380
he was overly confident in his outlook, produced
only a few tangible commercial results from

597
01:00:09,380 --> 01:00:15,530
his experiments, and had no qualms about irresponsibly
spending all of his investor’s money while

598
01:00:15,530 --> 01:00:20,900
still asking for more, a habit that would
irreparably damage his reputation in his later

599
01:00:20,900 --> 01:00:28,319
years and transform him into an impoverished
recluse who hid away in New York hotels.

600
01:00:28,319 --> 01:00:34,849
As he began to ail in the 1930s, Tesla enjoyed
a renewed wave of international popularity

601
01:00:34,849 --> 01:00:40,599
when he revealed that he was planning to assemble
a laser gun that he anticipated would be so

602
01:00:40,599 --> 01:00:46,960
powerful that it would end all conflict, yet
like many of his schemes it would never come

603
01:00:46,960 --> 01:00:53,670
to fruition, fizzling out by 1940 after his
last effort to interest the US government

604
01:00:53,670 --> 01:00:59,431
failed, although many decades after, curiosity
about the death ray would still persist. Following

605
01:00:59,431 --> 01:01:06,260
a period of mental and physical deterioration
in 1942, Nikola Tesla died at his room at

606
01:01:06,260 --> 01:01:12,799
the Hotel New Yorker on the 7th of January
1943, but was immortalized for his foundational

607
01:01:12,799 --> 01:01:15,930
contributions to science.

608
01:01:15,930 --> 01:01:20,640
Today one of the world’s most prominent
and valuable companies is called Tesla Inc.

609
01:01:20,640 --> 01:01:25,859
It is a company which seeks to revolutionize
transport systems and how energy is delivered

610
01:01:25,859 --> 01:01:31,500
across the world and while it has no direct
connections to Nikola Tesla the man, it is

611
01:01:31,500 --> 01:01:37,530
fitting that his life’s work has been remembered
in this way. Tesla, an ethnic Serb, hailed

612
01:01:37,530 --> 01:01:42,680
from Croatia in the mid-nineteenth century
at a time when the Balkans was very far from

613
01:01:42,680 --> 01:01:48,510
the centre of technological and industrial
development and formed part of the Austro-Hungarian

614
01:01:48,510 --> 01:01:54,430
Empire, a backwards power which did not contribute
greatly to wider societal development at the

615
01:01:54,430 --> 01:01:59,819
time. And yet, despite these impediments,
he made enormous breakthroughs during his

616
01:01:59,819 --> 01:02:05,920
lifetime in a wide range of different fields.
It was Tesla who first pioneered alternating

617
01:02:05,920 --> 01:02:11,760
current, who made major advances in radio
technology and who first devised many of the

618
01:02:11,760 --> 01:02:17,460
systems which are used today in renewable
energy systems. Perhaps most impressively,

619
01:02:17,460 --> 01:02:23,700
he devised the entire concept of wireless
technology and communication. In this respect

620
01:02:23,700 --> 01:02:29,380
he stands in a small line of individuals in
modern history which includes Galileo Galilei,

621
01:02:29,380 --> 01:02:36,760
Isaac Newton, Thomas Edison and Albert Einstein
whose life’s work have transformed our understanding

622
01:02:36,760 --> 01:02:41,020
of modern science and how society functions.

623
01:02:41,020 --> 01:02:45,920
Given all of this, we might ask why Tesla
was not credited as much as he should have

624
01:02:45,920 --> 01:02:51,539
been in his own lifetime for his successes?
The answer to this conundrum is relatively

625
01:02:51,539 --> 01:02:57,390
clear. Tesla’s ideas and the systems he
came up with, were simply ahead of their time

626
01:02:57,390 --> 01:03:01,970
and he was overshadowed by the innovations
introduced into western society by Thomas

627
01:03:01,970 --> 01:03:07,590
Edison, a similar genius whose designs around
electricity were more suitable for the level

628
01:03:07,590 --> 01:03:12,369
of technological development which prevailed
in Europe and North America in the second

629
01:03:12,369 --> 01:03:17,660
half of the nineteenth century. But while
many of Edison’s inventions and pioneering

630
01:03:17,660 --> 01:03:24,089
work around electricity generation are now
viewed as being somewhat archaic and inefficient,

631
01:03:24,089 --> 01:03:29,799
Tesla’s designs and ideas are still widely
praised. Perhaps in this respect it should

632
01:03:29,799 --> 01:03:35,160
be left to Edison to have the last word on
Tesla. While he dismissed the Serb’s idea

633
01:03:35,160 --> 01:03:40,700
of alternating current as being impractical
at the time, in the end Edison concluded that

634
01:03:40,700 --> 01:03:46,220
Tesla was one of the truly great figures in
the development of our electric world. Edison

635
01:03:46,220 --> 01:03:51,170
was entirely correct in this assessment. While
Edison won the contest to become the leading

636
01:03:51,170 --> 01:03:56,251
figure in electrification in North America
at the end of the nineteenth century, when

637
01:03:56,251 --> 01:04:01,940
you boil a kettle or use many other electrical
appliances today you are most likely using

638
01:04:01,940 --> 01:04:07,760
an alternating current device rather than
the direct current which Edison championed.

639
01:04:07,760 --> 01:04:13,000
As such, while Tesla lost the current war
in the nineteenth century, he was a prophet

640
01:04:13,000 --> 01:04:19,279
of twentieth and even twenty-first-century
technology and energy efficiency.

641
01:04:19,279 --> 01:04:25,010
Although Nikola Tesla did not invent or rather
discover AC power, his genius lies in his

642
01:04:25,010 --> 01:04:31,420
ability to find far-reaching real-world applications
to new discoveries and natural phenomena.

643
01:04:31,420 --> 01:04:37,300
He possessed a vision which few of his contemporaries
could match, even Thomas Edison, and there

644
01:04:37,300 --> 01:04:42,900
is no doubt that he changed the world, just
as much as his former employer, as the light

645
01:04:42,900 --> 01:04:48,589
bulb would be next to useless without a reliable
and safe means of power delivery over long

646
01:04:48,589 --> 01:04:54,849
distances. Indeed, there are few people whose
work has impacted more on modern day society

647
01:04:54,849 --> 01:04:59,859
than Nikola Tesla, with electric power now
being available to the majority of the human

648
01:04:59,859 --> 01:05:05,500
race, largely thanks to his work. He even
tried to work out a way in which, power could

649
01:05:05,500 --> 01:05:12,770
be delivered freely to consumers, which perhaps
both explains why he is today so highly regarded,

650
01:05:12,770 --> 01:05:17,750
but also why, he never managed to monetise
his work in the same manner as people like

651
01:05:17,750 --> 01:05:23,980
Edison. Perhaps what most endears us to people
like Nikola Tesla, Albert Einstein and the

652
01:05:23,980 --> 01:05:30,250
breed of latter-day inventors and scientists
who with little or no formal education revolutionised

653
01:05:30,250 --> 01:05:35,609
their respective fields, is the image of the
eccentric, mad scientist, working alone on

654
01:05:35,609 --> 01:05:41,809
experiments and calculations. In an age in
which the discoveries and achievements of

655
01:05:41,809 --> 01:05:47,330
individual scientists are today subsumed by
the multinational corporations they work for,

656
01:05:47,330 --> 01:05:50,710
even the one that bears Tesla’s name, there
is something romantic and inspiring about

657
01:05:50,710 --> 01:05:57,390
one human being having such a positive impact
on human civilization through their own endeavour.

658
01:05:57,390 --> 01:06:02,369
Nikola Tesla is one of these people and despite
the fact that he did not perhaps receive the

659
01:06:02,369 --> 01:06:09,140
money or recognition he deserved in his lifetime,
he has since his death been immortalised,

660
01:06:09,140 --> 01:06:15,539
as one of the greatest and most important
inventors to have ever lived.

661
01:06:15,539 --> 01:06:20,099
What do you think of Nikola Tesla? Do you
believe he was a more brilliant scientist

662
01:06:20,099 --> 01:06:26,059
than Einstein? Please let us know in the comment
section and in the meantime thank you very

663
01:06:26,059 --> 01:08:56,539
much for watching.

